"""Triton implementation of ``aten::_foreach_expm1_`` for Ascend 910B.

Design
------
The operator is a pure elementwise in-place map over a ``Tensor[]``.  The whole
list is therefore packed into as few launches as possible: the kernel receives
every tensor pointer as an ordinary argument (no device metadata buffer, no
host->device copy), the block boundaries between slots are computed on the host
and passed as scalars (so no program performs integer division), and the grid is
the sum of each slot's block count, so every program belongs to exactly one
tensor and never straddles two of them.

Three kernels, chosen by the shape of the work rather than by opcode:

* ``_expm1_single`` - one tensor, no slot dispatch at all.  Single-tensor
  workloads are dominated by the fixed per-launch cost, so this path stays as
  lean as possible in both arguments and device code.
* ``_expm1_fused_uni`` - a whole list of equal-length flat tensors in one
  launch with a small argument vector (slot from the grid axis, one shared n).
* ``_expm1_fused`` - the general heterogeneous list, with host-computed
  cumulative block boundaries so no program performs integer division.
* ``_expm1_strided`` - gappy views (``x[:, ::2]``), enumerated logically and
  mapped through the collapsed shape/stride list.

Flat addressable means contiguous, or non-overlapping-and-dense (e.g. a
transposed view): its storage span equals ``numel``, so enumerating
``[data_ptr, data_ptr + numel)`` visits every element exactly once.  Since
``expm1`` is position-wise, visiting memory order instead of logical order is
identical and coalescing is optimal.

Semantics preserved from ATen: in-place mutation, unchanged storage/strides,
``None`` return, ``RuntimeError`` for non-floating dtypes, for internally
overlapping (``expand``) tensors and for an empty list, and strictly sequential
application in list order when two list elements alias.
"""

